tests.description:
VERDICT: FAIL
REASON: The code performs size-dependent operations on attacker-controlled network input without adequate bounds checks. Specifically, it writes a NUL terminator at buffer[n] after an SSL_read() into a fixed-size stack buffer without ensuring n < PROTOCOLMSGLEN, allowing a one-byte write past the end of the buffer when the read fills the buffer; and it uses unbounded sscanf("%s") into fixed-size account buffers, allowing overflow/overflow-style memory corruption. These are reachable from attacker-controlled connections (server and client paths) and can cause crashes or disrupted protocol state.

EVIDENCE:
- Out-of-bounds NUL write in server receive path: [/repo/bankserver.c:154-163] shows the server zeroes the buffer, does n = SSL_read(ssl, buffer,PROTOCOLMSGLEN); checks only n < 0, then performs buffer[n] = 0. If SSL_read returns PROTOCOLMSGLEN (340), buffer[340] is written which is past the allocated buffer (valid indices 0..339). This is a direct size-check omission that can cause stack corruption/crash.
  - File: repo/bankserver.c lines 154-163
    - 154: bzero(buffer,PROTOCOLMSGLEN);
    - 156: n = SSL_read(ssl, buffer,PROTOCOLMSGLEN);
    - 158: if (n < 0) { ... }
    - 162: buffer[n] = 0;
- Same dangerous pattern in client receive path: [/repo/atmclient.c:170-178] the client also calls n = SSL_read(..., PROTOCOLMSGLEN) and then buffer[n] = 0 without checking n < PROTOCOLMSGLEN, allowing the identical off-by-one out-of-bounds write when n == PROTOCOLMSGLEN.
  - File: repo/atmclient.c lines 170-178
    - 170: bzero(buffer,PROTOCOLMSGLEN);
    - 171: n = SSL_read(ssl, buffer,PROTOCOLMSGLEN);
    - 173: if (n < 0) { ... }
    - 177: buffer[n] = 0;
- PROTOCOLMSGLEN and AUTH_IDBYTESIZE constants (showing sizes): [/repo/global.h:33-41]
  - PROTOCOLMSGLEN is defined as 340 and AUTH_IDBYTESIZE is 32. This makes it possible for SSL_read to return 340 and trigger the out-of-bounds write.
  - File: repo/global.h lines 33-41
    - 35: #define PROTOCOLMSGLEN 340
    - 41: #define AUTH_IDBYTESIZE 32
- Unbounded sscanf into account buffer (possible overflow / memory corruption): [/repo/bankcommand.c:48-51] uses sscanf(str1+AUTH_IDBYTESIZE,"#%s %c %ld %d%c", trans.account, ...) where %s has no width limit. trans.account has size MAXACCNAMELEN+1 (from [/repo/bank.h:48-51] and [/repo/global.h:24-26]); an attacker-controlled long field in the message (within PROTOCOLMSGLEN) can overflow trans.account before later length checks occur.
  - File: repo/bankcommand.c lines 48-51
    - 48: char end=1;
    - 49: sscanf(str1+AUTH_IDBYTESIZE,"#%s %c %ld %d%c", trans.account, &trans.dowhat, &trans.dollar, &trans.cent, &end);
    - 50: if (end!='#') return trans;
  - File: repo/bank.h lines 48-51 (definition of trans.account size)
    - 48: struct banktransdata { ...
    - 50: char account[MAXACCNAMELEN+1];
  - File: repo/global.h lines 23-26 (MAXACCNAMELEN)
    - 25: #define MAXACCNAMELEN 250

Why this fails the test case:
- The test requires that for every reachable attacker-influenced runtime interaction, the implementation must not throw uncaught exceptions or perform unchecked size-dependent operations resulting in crashes or state disruption. The code contains reachable network-facing code paths (server Servlet and client startclient) where attacker-controlled data is read into fixed-size buffers and then used in size-dependent ways without sufficient validation. The out-of-bounds NUL write and unbounded sscanf are concrete violations.

How an attacker can trigger it (concise):
- Connect to the server socket and send a payload of exactly PROTOCOLMSGLEN bytes. SSL_read may return PROTOCOLMSGLEN and subsequent buffer[n] = 0 writes one byte past the stack buffer, which can corrupt control data and crash the process. Alternatively, craft a message with an overly long account name field (within the allowed PROTOCOLMSGLEN message) to overflow trans.account via the unbounded %s in sscanf, leading to memory corruption/crash or disrupted server state.

Suggested fixes (not required for verdict):
- Ensure SSL_read return value n is checked and bounded before writing the NUL terminator: only write buffer[n]=0 if n < PROTOCOLMSGLEN; otherwise ensure the buffer is NUL-terminated at buffer[PROTOCOLMSGLEN-1] or reject reads of exactly PROTOCOLMSGLEN bytes.
- Replace unbounded sscanf("%s") with a bounded format specifier (e.g. "%250s") or use safer parsing functions.

I have provided code locations and sufficient static evidence of the vulnerabilities. No dynamic run was necessary to demonstrate the missing size checks and unbounded parsing that violate the test criteria.

